
`addressextract` parses an OpenStreetmap PBF file and exports all addresses
e.g. objects with `addr:*` tags on them. It also reassembles admin and postcode
boundarys and extends the addressinformation with those.

The input is an OpenStreetMap PBF (or OSM XML) file. The output is one JSON
document with a `meta` object (the timestamp of the input) and an `addresses` array.
The addresses are written one per line, so `grep` and `diff` work on them too.

A typical address looks like this (pretty printed from the Kreis Gütersloh extract):

    {
      "bbox": [8.4281104, 51.80841, 8.4281104, 51.80841],
      "city": "Rietberg",
      "country": "DE",
      "geomcity": "Rietberg",
      "geompostcode": "33397",
      "geomsuburb": "Rietberg",
      "housenumber": "2",
      "id": 259778720,
      "lat": 51.80841,
      "lon": 8.4281104,
      "postcode": "33397",
      "source": "node",
      "street": "Bolzenmarkt"
    }

All the `geom*` tags (`geomcity`, `geomcounty`, `geomsuburb`, `geompostcode`)
originate from the boundaries, be it postcode or admin boundary - they are what the
coordinates of the object fall into, independent of the `addr:*` tags the mapper set.
`id` and `source` (`node` or `way`) tell the origin osm object and its id. `lat` and `lon`
are the position (the centroid for ways), `bbox` is `[minlon, minlat, maxlon, maxlat]`.

Additionally you can run the extractor with `-e` so it will check the address and
add an `errors` field describing problems with it. It could look like this
(`bbox`, `lat` and `lon` left out):

    {
      "city": "Halle (Westf.)",
      "country": "DE",
      "errors": [
        "No housenumber",
        "No addr:street or addr:place"
      ],
      "geomcity": "Halle (Westf.)",
      "geompostcode": "33790",
      "geomsuburb": "Halle (Westf)",
      "id": 307194541,
      "postcode": "33790",
      "source": "node"
    }

Usage
=====

	addressextract [options] -i mylocal.pbf -o addresses.json

Without `-e` and `-m` only the addresses are exported as they are tagged. Options:

	-i, --infile arg        Input file (PBF or OSM XML)
	-o, --outfile arg       Output file
	-e, --errors            Add geom* tags and check the address for errors
	-m, --missing           Add geom* tags only, without the error analysis
	-v, --verbose           Print index statistics to stdout
	    --nocache           Do not use the boundary cache grid (slow, same result)
	    --postcoderegex arg Postcode validation regex, default ^[0-9]{5}$
	-h, --help              Show the options

Progress and diagnostics go to stderr. The run takes three passes over the input
file: relations, then boundaries/postcodes/buildings, then the addresses. A
Regierungsbezirk sized extract (about 170 MB) takes roughly 20 seconds with `-e`.

Filtering and extracting
========================

To filter or extract addresses from the resulting json there is either the perl script
`addrfilter` in this repository:

	addrfilter -p 33330 -i owl.json -c

To export all address with postcode 33330 and dump it as CSV. An alternative is to use `jq`:

	jq -r '.addresses[] | [ .id, .source, .postcode, .city, .street, .housenumber ] | @csv' owl.json

Build
=====

Needs a C++17 compiler, cmake 3.19 or newer, Boost, GDAL and the header only
libraries libosmium, protozero and nlohmann/json.

With the Debian (trixie or newer) packages:

	apt-get install build-essential cmake libboost-dev libboost-program-options-dev \
	    libgdal-dev libbz2-dev libexpat1-dev zlib1g-dev \
	    libosmium2-dev libprotozero-dev nlohmann-json3-dev

	git clone https://github.com/flohoff/addressextract.git
	cd addressextract
	cmake -S . -B build
	cmake --build build -j

Alternatively get the header only dependencies from git instead of the packages -
if you need a newer version than the distribution has. Cloned next to the
source (`libosmium`, `protozero`, `json`) or as siblings (`../libosmium`,
`../protozero`) they are found without further configuration:

	cd addressextract
	git clone https://github.com/osmcode/libosmium
	git clone https://github.com/mapbox/protozero
	git clone https://github.com/nlohmann/json

A system installed `nlohmann-json3-dev` takes precedence over the `json` clone.

The binary is `build/addressextract`. For the unit tests install `catch2` (plus `gcovr`
for coverage and `osmium-tool` for the PBF test) - see "Tests" below.

Example run on an extract from Geofabrik:

	wget https://download.geofabrik.de/europe/germany/nordrhein-westfalen/detmold-regbez-latest.osm.pbf -O owl.pbf
	build/addressextract -e -i owl.pbf -o owl.json

Search as you type
==================

Using Debian/Buster to setup a solr:

	sudo apt-get install solr-jetty jq
	sudo cp -rav searchasyoutype/solr36conf/* /etc/solr/
	sudo service jetty9 restart

	addressextract -m -i mylittlepbf -o addresses.json
    jq .addresses <addresses.json >onlyaddresses.json

	searchasyoutype/pushtosolr http://localhost:8080/solr onlyaddresses.json

Now you can query the solr for results:

	curl http://localhost:8080/solr/address/?q=postcode:3333%20street:Heidestra%C3%9Fe

Typical query time is less than 50ms and the result looks like this:

    {
      "responseHeader": {
        "status": 0,
        "QTime": 13,
        "params": {
          "q": "postcode:33330 street:Heidestraße"
        }
      },
      "response": {
        "numFound": 26262,
        "start": 0,
        "docs": [
          {
            "city": "Gütersloh",
            "geomcity": "Gütersloh",
            "geompostcode": "33330",
            "housenumber": "1c",
            "id": "7380458133",
            "postcode": "33330",
            "street": "Heidestraße"
          },
    [ ... ]

